# Line endings are part of the contract here, not incidental formatting.
#
# Without this file, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true (the git-for-
# Windows default) rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. That is
# harmless for most of them and fatal for anything a POSIX shell parses: a
# 2026-08-25 Windows ARM64 re-validation found all 40 tracked .sh files
# checked out as CRLF, so run_tests.sh died with
#     syntax error near unexpected token `$'do\r''
# and every installer component was broken the same way under Git Bash or
# WSL2. The blobs in the repo were correct LF throughout; only the checkout
# was wrong, and nothing in the repo asserted otherwise.
#
# After this lands, existing clones need one renormalization:
#     git add --renormalize . && git checkout -- .

* text=auto

# LF-only: parsed by a POSIX shell, by launchd, or by an interpreter that
# reads the shebang line -- a trailing CR there is a syntax error.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.plist         text eol=lf
*.applescript   text eol=lf
installers/lib/hooks/pre-commit text eol=lf

# CRLF: the Windows PowerShell layer, which is Windows-only by definition.
# Note this is about line endings only -- the same files are also held
# ASCII-only, for a different reason, by
# Templates/Scripts/tests/test_static.py::TestPowerShellEncoding.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psd1          text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch, never diff as text.
*.png           binary
*.pdf           binary
